# Cameras, streams and snapshots

Say what the person is looking at: a live stream, a snapshot or nothing yet. The viewer’s connection, a still image’s capture time and the camera entity’s reported state are three separate facts. Show each only from evidence, and keep the picture as the camera supplied it, without recolouring or obscuring it.

These examples are simulated with invented data. They do not open a camera or control a home. This page covers the design contract (HA-CTRL-015, from gap HA-GAP-015). It does not add a viewer.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-015-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Source capability | Show |
| --- | --- |
| Still image | Label it a snapshot. Use a supplied capture time. A fetch time is not a capture time. |
| Playable stream | Keep the native playback, fullscreen and audio controls where supported. Label loading, connected and failed from what the viewer reports. |
| Recording state | Show only a state the entity reports. Seeing a stream does not show recording, and Idle can mean the integration does not report activity. |
| Privacy or power control | Offer only a control the camera supports, and say what it affects. Closing a viewer does not necessarily stop the camera. |
| Credentials and image URL | Keep native authorisation. Keep tokens, private URLs and household images out of public examples. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-015-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Never put a Live label on a retained snapshot or a stalled image. If playback stalls, keep the native error and label any remaining still with its known capture time. If no capture time is supplied, say “Capture time not provided”. Do not use the page load time.

A stream failure, denied permission, unavailable entity and privacy mode are different conditions. Give the supplied reason and a recovery step that works. Retrying playback must not turn off privacy mode, start recording or bypass sign-in. Closing the viewer ends that viewing surface only as far as the native viewer says. It does not mean the physical camera stops streaming or recording.

Avoid automatic audio or motion on shared screens. One still image cannot show that a doorway is clear or that nobody is there.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-015-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Put the camera name and viewing state outside the image, or on a surface with proven contrast. Write “Snapshot captured at…” only for a capture time. A decorative placeholder needs no invented description. If the image carries essential information, give it alternative text for the task ([WG-082](../content/formatting-content/images.html#wg-082)). A camera name does not describe everything in a live video.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-015-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Keep native media controls named and keyboard-operable. Check that leaving fullscreen returns focus, and that controls stay visible over light and dark frames. Give the viewer an accessible camera name and a text state. If the task needs event information, point to a separate supported text source. Do not invent live audio description, captions or incident detection. Respect reduced motion and offer a way to stop non-essential playback where the native viewer allows it. Record any limit you find.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-015-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Use the native Camera and stream implementation. The entity can report Idle while a feed is visible, on integrations that do not report activity. The examples use an invented diagram and static states. A future camera adapter must keep authentication, the stream lifecycle and the native controls. It must also document the privacy and accessible-media limits for the integration and browser it was tested with.

